Stop the PulseAudio sink from providing the pipeline clock (WEBKIT_GST_AUDIO_SYSTEM_CLOCK).

On the Xperia 10 II (libhybris PulseAudio) the GStreamer pulsesink that WebKit
builds via createAutoAudioSink intermittently locks up: its provided clock
(GstPulseSinkClock) freezes at the current playback instant and never advances.
Because the audio sink is the pipeline's clock provider, the whole pipeline —
which is slaved to that clock — stalls. Audio plays out only the already-buffered
~250 ms and then goes silent, and the picture freezes with it, while the
HTMLMediaElement still reports paused=false / muted=false.

Device-reproduced on YouTube (build 354): a real unmute (or an MSE audio re-init)
froze currentTime at the exact unmute timestamp; GST_DEBUG showed
GstPulseSinkClock internal time pinned while wall-clock advanced, with the render
thread spinning in gst_system_clock_id_wait (... unlocked after timeout ...
restart). At the PulseAudio level the active sink-input was left in state CORKED
even though the element was playing+unmuted. This is a cork/clock deadlock: the
pulse stream cannot uncork until the audio clock advances, but the audio clock
cannot advance until the stream uncorks. It is intermittent (a race, worsened by
the device's heavy memory pressure) — sometimes the stream uncorks and audio
plays, sometimes it does not.

Forcing provide-clock=FALSE on the pulsesink makes the pipeline select the
monotonic GstSystemClock instead, so the pipeline clock keeps advancing
regardless of the pulse stream state and the deadlock cannot form; pulsesink
resamples (its default slave-method=skew) to track the audio HW clock. Gated by
WEBKIT_GST_AUDIO_SYSTEM_CLOCK (set in deploy/runtime-common.sh). Unset = upstream
behaviour. Set in the same autoaudiosink "child-added" hook that the media.role
override (webkit-gst-media-role-env.patch) uses, so it applies to every browser
audio sink before the pipeline reaches PAUSED/PLAYING and picks its clock.
--- a/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/gstreamer/GStreamerCommon.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/gstreamer/GStreamerCommon.cpp
@@ -1189,6 +1189,23 @@
             auto& clientName = getApplicationName();
             g_object_set(object, "client-name", clientName.ascii().data(), nullptr);
         }
+        // SFOS: do not let the PulseAudio sink provide the pipeline clock. On the
+        // Xperia 10 II (libhybris PulseAudio) the pulsesink clock intermittently
+        // freezes after its stream is (re)corked across a mute/unmute or an MSE
+        // re-init: the stream cannot uncork until the audio clock advances, but the
+        // clock cannot advance until the stream uncorks — a deadlock. The pipeline,
+        // slaved to that frozen clock, then stalls (audio cuts out ~250 ms in and
+        // the picture freezes; device-reproduced on YouTube, sink-input left CORKED
+        // while the element reports playing+unmuted). Forcing provide-clock=FALSE
+        // makes the pipeline run off the monotonic system clock, breaking the cycle;
+        // pulsesink resamples (slave-method=skew) to track the audio HW clock. Gated
+        // by WEBKIT_GST_AUDIO_SYSTEM_CLOCK (set in deploy/runtime-common.sh); unset =
+        // upstream behaviour.
+        if (g_object_class_find_property(objectClass, "provide-clock")) {
+            const char* sysClock = std::getenv("WEBKIT_GST_AUDIO_SYSTEM_CLOCK");
+            if (sysClock && *sysClock && g_strcmp0(sysClock, "0"))
+                g_object_set(object, "provide-clock", FALSE, nullptr);
+        }
     }), effectiveRole.isolatedCopy().releaseImpl().leakRef(), static_cast<GClosureNotify>([](gpointer userData, GClosure*) {
         if (auto* roleImpl = reinterpret_cast<StringImpl*>(userData))
             roleImpl->deref();
